 granted city status in 1170. Due to its location at the foot of the Erzgebirge (literally ore mountains), in the 16th century Chemnitz began to grow in size an importance as a place of trade, and later as the base of industrial production. The increase in Saxon coal mining during the 18th century allowed Chemnitz to develop into one of the most important centres of the German machine and textile industries - factors which gave it the nickname of \"little Manchester\".","grounding":[{"source":"wikivoyage","title":"Chemnitz","url":"https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki/Chemnitz","licence":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"short":"Chemnitz is a large city in Germany and a European Capital of Culture. The Karl Marx Monument is the landmark, Museum Gunzenhauser holds the art, and ten hours covers the centre plus the Kuchwald. May through September is the comfortable stretch.","short_src":"rewrite","facts":{"population":247220,"visit_h":7.3}},"do":[{"name":"Sächsisches Industriemuseum","type":"activity","sources":["wikivoyage"],"evidence":{"method":"open","n_sources":1,"sources":["wikivoyage"],"curated":"wikivoyage_do"}},{"name":"Volkswagen Motorenwerk Chemnitz","type":"activity","sources":["wikivoyage"],"evidence":{"method":"open","n_sources":1,"sources":["wikivoyage"],"curated":"wikivoyage_do"}},{"name":"Chemnitzer FC","type":"activity","sources":["wikivoyage"],"evidence":{"method":"open","n_sources":1,"sources":["wikivoyage"],"curated":"wikivoyage_do"}},{"name":"Chemnitz 99","type":"activity","sources":["wikivoyage"],"evidence":{"method":"open","n_sources":1,"sources":["wikivoyage"],"curated":"wikivoyage_do"}},{"name":"Bogensportclub Chemnitz-Rabenstein e.V.","type":"activity","sources":["wikivoyage"],"evidence":{"method":"open","n_sources":1,"sources":["wikivoyage"],"curated":"wikivoyage_do"}},{"name":"CineStar","type":"activity","sources":["wikivoyage"],"evidence":{"method":"open","n_sources":1,"sources":["wikivoyage"],"curated":"wikivoyage_do"}}],"nearby":{"lakes":[{"layer":"lakes","cc":